      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Squares made of pieces"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "Imagine a square garden whose side is split into two parts, a and b. The garden breaks into four pieces: a square a by a, a square b by b and two rectangles a by b. Counting these pieces gives an algebraic identity."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "(a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2",
          "(a - b)^2 = a^2 - 2ab + b^2",
          "103 x 97 = 9991",
          "a^2 - b^2 = (a + b)(a - b)",
          "check with numbers",
          "do not drop 2ab",
          "factorising",
          "identity"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "An identity is an equation that is true for all values of the variables, unlike an ordinary equation that is true only for some values.\nCutting a square of side (a + b) into pieces gives (a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2.\nReplacing b by -b gives (a - b)^2 = a^2 - 2ab + b^2.\nThe difference of squares identity is a^2 - b^2 = (a + b)(a - b). A rectangle cut and rearranged shows it.\nWe use identities to calculate quickly: 103 x 97 = (100 + 3)(100 - 3) = 10000 - 9 = 9991.\nThey also help to factorise: x^2 + 6x + 9 = (x + 3)^2 and x^2 - 16 = (x + 4)(x - 4).\nTo check an identity, test it with numbers, for example a = 2 and b = 1 gives 9 on both sides of (a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2.\nA common mistake is to write (a + b)^2 = a^2 + b^2. The middle term 2ab is always there."
        }
      },
